   116	- **Pass.** A round passes when both changes against the previous round (attacker best training value; reselected
   117	  reference mean normalised profit) are below the registered threshold 0.005. Stop at the first pass; cap three rounds
   118	  (four times the registered restarts in all). Reported as "passed the registered incremental check within three rounds"
   119	  or "not passed at the cap"; this is not convergence, not optimality, and not a bound on the remaining search error.
   120	- **Winner.** The best training value over all rounds, evaluated on the 200 evaluation seeds against the unchanged frozen
   121	  reference.
   122	
   123	## 3. Analysis (fixed now)
   124	
   125	Per cell: the frozen and final gains, the rounds and their changes, the round-1 comparison, the status. Q1 (C* - R3,
   126	D2+H3) and Q4 (B3 - R3, D2+H3), per fresh market and pooled, with the final winners substituted for the re-searched cells
   127	and the frozen winners elsewhere, beside the frozen values (`recompute`; only validated full-run records with this run's
   128	binding are substituted). Per market and pooled nominal paired percentile intervals (9,999 resamples, seed 0),
   129	conditional on these six markets and the selected policies and references, without search or market-sampling
   130	uncertainty and without registered calibration. Q1 under X7-SC is recomputed the same way. Only this run's cells enter, each with its complete binding, its identity,
   131	finite gains, and the evaluation seeds' length; any other result in the directory is ignored. Descriptive; no decision
   132	is recomputed.
   133	
   134	## 4. Expectations (written before any continuation result)
   135	
   136	None on direction. Reported: how many of the 19 cells pass within three rounds, each nesting cell's shortfall before and after, and the largest change of a Q1 or Q4
   137	market difference.
   138	
   139	## 5. Execution contract
   140	
   141	`docs/revision/followup2/convergence_v2.py` (under `docs/`, code freeze). Before any episode: `TOUCHSTONE_LOCKED` refused,
   142	`experiments/locked.yaml` and the manifest equal protocol-v2; salt, commit, runner and `sim/` equal to protocol-v2;
   143	configuration hashes (fresh: recorded container hashes; headline: the registered record's); each job paired with its
   144	frozen reference; each job's budget equal to its frozen search record's. Inputs copied and checked against the original
   145	freeze. Each result binds to its input hashes, job provenance, smoke flag, stopping settings, and driver hash; a saved
   146	result is reused only under the same binding and refused otherwise. Inventory: every output with its hash, Run B's
   147	deviations, hashes of `convergence_v2.py`, `common.py`, `guards.py`, the imported helpers, this plan, and the report, the
   148	run arguments, the environment. A full run is refused unless every `--evidence` file reports a pass.
   149	
   150	**Gate evidence.** Measured on 2026-11-11 (Mac, full budget): round 1 of fresh1 F111 D2+H3 differs from the registered
   151	pod record (held-out gains by up to 0.0204, changes by 0.0034), and the frozen doubling stage itself
   152	(`reset_followup_v2.py --reproduce-doubling`, `results/v2/followup2-diag-dbl-fresh1`) differs from it by exactly the same
   153	amounts; headline F111 X7-SC differs similarly (gains 0.0221). Round 1 therefore equals the frozen stage, and flagged
   154	cells diverge between the Mac and the pods: a small arithmetic difference sends the doubled search elsewhere in these
   155	rugged cells. The gate is split accordingly. `convergence_v2.py --gate --site mac --cells headline@rho0.2|F111|X7-SC|profit,
   156	fresh1@rho0.2|F111|D2+H3|profit` on the Mac (free) compares round 1 with the frozen doubling stage run on the same machine
   157	(the driver reproduces the frozen code); the same with `--site pod`, first on the pod, compares round 1 with the
   158	registered record (the pod reproduces the registered run). Each writes `gate/<cell>__<site>.json` with the comparison,
   159	round 1's own values, the binding (full-budget provenance, gate settings, input hashes, driver, dependency hashes), and
   160	the environment. The main run is refused unless both cells have a passing Mac record and a passing pod record from this
   161	machine's numerical stack, each with exactly the binding recomputed now. If
   162	the pod gate fails, the run stops and is reported; the frozen estimates stay primary.
   163	
   164	## 6. Cost and spending controls
   165	
   166	`python3 docs/revision/followup2/cost_estimate.py`: up to 29.48 core-hours, 3.18 USD at 0.108 USD per core-hour (19 cells x
   167	3 rounds priced as registered doubling checks plus a held-out evaluation each round); the gate cells run serially, 1.06
   168	hours; billed path 3.60 hours (gate, an X7-SC cell's three rounds with 16 workers starting the 9 X7-SC cells first, 0.3 h
   169	preparation and tail) = 2.01 USD at 0.56 USD per hour. Estimate 3.30 USD (x 1.25 = 4.12); **stop cap
   170	4.25 USD**; **reservation 5.525 USD** (1.3 x the stop cap, the watchdog bound; amendment above) in ledger category `followup`
   171	(cap 72 USD shared by all follow-up runs; `budget_followup.py reserve`). A paid run requires `--started-at <allocation time>`, a positive rate, and the launcher's attempts file
   172	(`--attempts`). Admission is checked before each gate cell and before every round of every
   173	cell, including its first, with the round's estimate plus its evaluation and a 0.1-hour shutdown allowance; a running round
   174	is never killed (author's instruction). A cell refused admission, or stopped by it after a completed round, keeps its frozen estimate as primary and is
   175	reported "stopped at the cost cap"; any partial result is kept under `partial` and never substituted; after the last running round finishes, the pod is deleted and verified. This is an admission limit, not a
   176	billing ceiling.
   177	
   178	**Launcher handoff (draft 5).** The launcher copy pushes to the pod, before every paid driver call, its current
   179	`<name>.attempts` file and the ledger, and passes `--attempts`, `--ledger`, `--run-tag`, `--started-at` (the current pod's
   180	allocation time), `--usd-per-hour` (the price ceiling MAXCPH), `--stop-file /root/STOP`, and `--soft-deadline` (its soft
  cells end, so one worker killed with signal 9 (likely out of memory: 16 workers, 29 GB) lost 3.6 h of work and about
  3.43 USD. Three review rounds missed it; no test killed a worker, and no step measured memory per worker. Guard (for
  the rerun, tests first): a test that kills one worker and checks that finished cells are on disk and skipped on resume;
  a Mac-measured peak memory per cell that sets the worker count, checked by the launcher against the pod's memory.

- 2026-11-14, Run B rerun: I edited the launcher (`followup2_pod.sh`) before its harness had the new scenarios, so the
  first harness run could not show that the new scenarios fail on the old code. Mitigation: a red run of the new harness
  against the installed old launcher (`results/pods/logs_harness/f2_harness_red.log`) before the new launcher is
  installed. Guard (written rule): for a launcher change, install nothing until a red harness log against the installed
  copy exists; the review brief names that log, and a brief without it is not sent.
- 2026-11-14, Run B rerun: the worker check named in the guard above is a fixed count (7, from the Mac peak of 2.39 GB
  per cell, `memprobe/rss.log`) and a launcher refusal outside 1 to 16, not a live check of the pod's memory.
- 2026-11-15, Run B rerun review round 1 (both seats reject): my first fix saved cells in the parent, so a result in
  transfer could still be lost when a sibling died; a pool broken during submission escaped the handler; one worker ran
  the cell inside the driver, so a kill took the driver too; the restart estimate was a fixed 2.60 h, not the remaining
  cells. Guard (tests): `test_a_cell_that_ends_just_before_a_sibling_dies_is_already_on_disk`,
  `test_a_pool_broken_during_submission_is_a_lost_worker_not_a_crash`,
  `test_one_worker_still_runs_each_cell_in_its_own_process`, `test_restart_hours_schedule_the_lost_cells_longest_first...`,
  and the harness checks `run=lostnoest` and `--hours 2.75`, each red before the fix
  (`results/pods/logs_harness/f2_harness_red2.log`, `f2_harness_red3.log`). Written rule: a red run filtered with ONLY
  must also include the scenario that makes the remote script, or its remote checks fail for the wrong reason.
- 2026-11-15, Run B rerun: I called 7 workers safe from a round-1-only probe (2.39 GB). The full three-round probe
  peaked at 3.53 GB (`results/pods/memprobe3/rss.log`, 2 s sampling), so 7 workers would have run out of memory again. Guard (written
  rule): a memory figure that sets a worker count comes from the longest configuration the run can reach (all rounds),
  and the launcher's `PEAKGB` names that probe log.

